const intro={"n":22,"title":"Picardía Becomes a Cardsharp: Gambling and Deception","nums":[881,882,900],"summary":"Picardía recounts returning after years away and being enrolled in the National Guard. He explains how he learned to manipulate cards and gambling arrangements, including a partnership with an innkeeper who helped him profit from unsuspecting players. He describes several tricks across card games and other wagers. At the end, however, he calls gambling a harmful vice, says he has quit and argues that learning a bad habit can cost more than learning to work.","people":"Picardía as the narrator and gambler; the innkeeper who collaborates with him; other players who lose money; the National Guard mentioned at the opening.","themes":"Cunning versus honest work; gambling and social exploitation; the confession of wrongdoing; the instability of luck; critique of vice.","words":"naipe (playing card); baraja (deck); monte (card game); truco (card game); taba (traditional betting game with a bone); Guardia Nacional (National Guard).","questions":["What happens to Picardía when he returns?","How does the innkeeper contribute to his dishonest winnings?","Which details show that the outcome is arranged rather than left to luck?","Why should the tricks be read as the character's account rather than recommendations?","What judgment does Picardía make about gambling at the canto's conclusion?"],"analysis":"This canto describes cheating but closes with an explicit moral reflection against the gambling trade. Treating the passage as practical advice would ignore its narrative context. Its themes connect to the poem's distinction between clever survival and behavior that harms others.","related":"/en/library/old-vizcacha-vs-martin-fierro/","relatedTitle":"Prudence, cunning and ethics elsewhere in the poem"};
